import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var i={superfluidHelium:{theory:{concept:"[[Superfluidity is the ability of a liquid to flow with no viscosity at all \u2014 without any internal friction.]] Liquid helium-4 becomes superfluid below 2.17 K. By the two-fluid theory it then behaves like a mixture of two liquids: a normal part with viscosity and a superfluid part without it, and the colder the helium, the larger the superfluid part. In 1946 the Georgian physicist Elephter Andronikashvili measured these parts with a stack of thin discs swinging in the helium: the discs drag along only the normal part, so the period of their swing shows how much of the helium is normal and how much is superfluid."},section:{helium:{title:"Helium that does not freeze",text:"Helium is the only substance that stays liquid down to absolute zero at ordinary pressure (it freezes only under about 25 atmospheres). Helium-4 turns liquid at 4.2 K and boils violently, like water. At 2.172 K \u2014 the \u03BB-point \u2014 something remarkable happens: the boiling suddenly stops, the liquid becomes perfectly calm, and it turns into helium II. It flows through cracks too narrow for any other liquid, creeps up the walls of its vessel, and conducts heat better than any metal."},twoFluids:{title:"Two fluids in one",text:"The theory of Lev Landau (and, earlier, L\xE1szl\xF3 Tisza) describes helium II as if two liquids were mixed in the same space. The normal component behaves like an ordinary viscous liquid and carries the heat. [[The superfluid component has no viscosity at all and cannot be dragged by anything.]] The total density is \u03C1 = \u03C1\u2099 + \u03C1\u209B. As the temperature falls, the share of the superfluid grows: \u03C1\u2099/\u03C1 \u2248 (T/T_\u03BB)^5.6, so near 1 K the helium is almost entirely superfluid. The two-fluid picture is not two kinds of atoms: every atom takes part in both."},experiment:{title:"Andronikashvili's experiment",text:"A pile of very thin discs, spaced a fraction of a millimetre apart, hangs on a thin elastic fibre in the helium bath. Twisted a little, it swings back and forth like a torsion pendulum, with the period P = 2\u03C0\xB7\u221A(I/\u03BA), where I is its moment of inertia and \u03BA the stiffness of the fibre. The discs are so close that the viscous normal fluid between them is dragged along and turns with them; the superfluid, which has no viscosity, stays at rest. So the swinging system has the inertia of the discs plus only the normal helium: I = I\u2080 + (\u03C1\u2099/\u03C1)\xB7I_He."},result:{title:"What the period shows",text:"Above the \u03BB-point all the helium is normal, and the period is constant. Below it the period falls: part of the helium has been left behind, so there is less to swing. [[The period at a given temperature gives the share of the normal fluid directly: \u03C1\u2099/\u03C1 = (P\xB2 \u2212 P\u209B\xB2)/(P\u2099\xB2 \u2212 P\u209B\xB2), where P\u2099 is the period above the \u03BB-point and P\u209B the period when all the helium is superfluid.]] The measured curve confirmed the two-fluid theory of Tisza and Landau \u2014 Landau received the Nobel Prize in 1962 for his theory of liquid helium. In the simulation a cooling and warming run draws the same curve."},gap:{title:"Why the discs must be so close",text:"The normal fluid between two oscillating discs follows them only if the gap is thinner than the viscous penetration depth \u03B4 = \u221A(2\u03B7/(\u03C1\u2099\xB7\u03C9)): the thickness of the layer that viscosity can drag along. Here \u03B4 is a fraction of a millimetre. If the gap is wider, the fluid in the middle stays behind, only a layer of thickness \u03B4 moves with each disc, and the measurement gives a wrong value (and the friction changes the damping of the swing). That is why Andronikashvili used a stack of discs with gaps of about 0.2 mm. Try a gap of 1 mm and watch the measured value go wrong."},andronikashvili:{title:"Elephter Andronikashvili",text:"Elephter Andronikashvili (1910\u20131989), born in Tbilisi, was one of the leading Georgian physicists, an academician and for decades the head of the Institute of Physics in Tbilisi, which now bears his name. He carried out the experiment with the torsion pendulum of discs in liquid helium in 1946, and it became one of the classic experiments of low-temperature physics. It was the first direct measurement of the density of the normal component of helium II, and it is described in textbooks all over the world. This is why the topic is marked as important."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Why does the period of the pendulum fall below the \u03BB-point? (The superfluid does not turn with the discs, so there is less inertia.) What happens to the period in an ordinary liquid when you cool it? (Nothing: there is no superfluid component.) Why must the discs be close together? (So that the normal fluid between them is dragged along completely.) What does the period at 1.0 K tell about the helium? (Almost all of it is superfluid.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{density:"The two components of helium II",fraction:"The share of the normal fluid below the \u03BB-point (a good fit to the measurements)",period:"The period of the torsion pendulum (I: moment of inertia, \u03BA: stiffness of the fibre)",inertia:"The swinging inertia: the discs plus only the normal helium between them",analysis:"The share of the normal fluid from the periods (P\u2099 above the \u03BB-point, P\u209B when all is superfluid)",depth:"The viscous penetration depth: how far the viscosity can drag the normal fluid (\u03B7: viscosity)",damping:"Damping of the swing by the viscous normal fluid (G: how well the fluid follows the discs)"},variable:{T:"Temperature of the bath",P:"Period of the torsion pendulum",rho:"Densities of the normal and superfluid components",delta:"Viscous penetration depth",kappa:"Stiffness of the torsion fibre"},assumption:{stack:"Only the helium between the discs is counted, and the viscosity of the normal fluid is taken as constant (3 \xB5Pa\xB7s); the fibre and the suspension are ideal.",fit:"The share of the normal fluid follows the formula (T/T_\u03BB)^5.6, not the measured curve point by point; the torsion constant is chosen so that the period above the \u03BB-point is 2.5 s.",small:"The twist is small, so the superfluid stays below its critical velocity and no vortices appear. When the swing dies away the discs are twisted again.",scale:"Not to scale: the number of discs and the gaps are drawn schematically, the dots stand for the two components of the helium, and the angle of the swing is exaggerated six times."},param:{liquid:"Liquid in the bath",mode:"Temperature",temperature:"Temperature of the bath",discs:"Number of discs",gap:"Gap between the discs",thickness:"Thickness of a disc",sweepTime:"Time to cool from 2.6 K to 1.1 K",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{liquid:"Helium-4, or an ordinary liquid for comparison: it has no superfluid component.",mode:"The bath can cool down and warm up by itself, or stay at the temperature you choose.",temperature:"The \u03BB-point is at 2.172 K: below it helium becomes superfluid.",discs:"More discs mean more helium dragged along, and a bigger effect.",gap:"The gap must be thinner than the viscous penetration depth. Andronikashvili used about 0.2 mm.",thickness:"Thin discs carry less inertia of their own, so the helium counts for more.",sweepTime:"How long the bath takes to cool down; it then warms up again in the same time."},liquid:{helium:"Liquid helium-4",ordinary:"An ordinary liquid"},preset:{sweep:"Cools down and warms up by itself",fixed:"Stays where I put it",coldest:"1.0 K",cold:"1.5 K",middle:"1.9 K",nearLambda:"2.1 K",aboveLambda:"2.4 K",few:"10",some:"25",many:"50",close:"0.10 mm",original:"0.21 mm",wide:"0.5 mm",veryWide:"1 mm"},measure:{temperature:"Temperature of the bath",period:"Period of the pendulum",normalFraction:"Normal fluid (true value)",superFraction:"Superfluid",periodShift:"Change of the period from the one above the \u03BB-point",normalFromPeriod:"Normal fluid found from the period",angle:"Angle of the twist",penetration:"Viscous penetration depth",coupling:"How well the normal fluid follows the discs",damping:"Damping rate of the swing, 1/s",inertiaRatio:"Inertia of the helium compared with the rigid parts",normalDensity:"Density of the normal fluid",superDensity:"Density of the superfluid"},view:{above:"Above the \u03BB-point: helium I, an ordinary liquid",below:"Below the \u03BB-point: helium II, partly superfluid",ordinary:"An ordinary liquid: never superfluid",temperature:"T = {temperature} \xB7 the \u03BB-point is {lambda}",period:"Period of the pendulum: {period}",discs:"{count} discs, gaps of {gap}",fractions:"Normal {normal} \xB7 superfluid {superfluid}",topView:"One gap from above (angle \xD7{scale})",normalLegend:"Big dots: normal helium (turns with the disc)",superLegend:"Small dots: superfluid (stays at rest)"},graph:{period:"Period against temperature (the Andronikashvili curve)",fractions:"Normal and superfluid shares against temperature",swing:"The swing of the pendulum against time"},challenge:{aboveLambda:{title:"Above the \u03BB-point",description:"Hold the bath of helium above the \u03BB-point, where there is no superfluid at all.",hint:'Choose "Stays where I put it" and a temperature above 2.172 K.'},mostlySuper:{title:"Almost all superfluid",description:"Hold the bath so that at least 90 % of the helium is superfluid.",hint:"The share of the superfluid grows as the temperature falls: about 1.35 K or colder."},half:{title:"Half and half",description:"Find a temperature at which the helium is between 45 % and 55 % superfluid.",hint:"\u03C1\u2099/\u03C1 = (T/T_\u03BB)^5.6 equals one half at about 1.92 K."},control:{title:"A control experiment",description:"Choose an ordinary liquid and cool it to 1.5 K or less: show that the period does not change at all.",hint:"An ordinary liquid has no superfluid component to be left behind."},measureIt:{title:"Measure the normal fluid",description:"With a gap of at most 0.15 mm and a bath below 2 K, make the normal share found from the period agree with the true one to within 2 percentage points.",hint:"The gap must be thinner than the penetration depth."},wideGap:{title:"Spoil the experiment",description:"Choose a gap of at least 0.8 mm so that the share found from the period is wrong by 10 percentage points or more (the bath between 1.3 K and 2 K).",hint:"A wide gap lets the fluid in the middle stay behind."},bigEffect:{title:"A big effect",description:"Design the apparatus so that at 1.3 K or colder the period is at least 23 % shorter than above the \u03BB-point.",hint:"Make the helium count as much as possible: the most discs (60), the thinnest ones (0.005 mm) and a gap of about 0.25 mm."}}}},o={sim:i};export{o as default,i as sim};
